<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Endeavors DCM Case Tracker — Welcome Packet</title>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins:wght@400;500;600;700;800&family=Open+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;1,400&display=swap');

  /* ---- Style knobs (match the app) ---- */
  :root{
    --navy:#1a3a5c; --navy-deep:#0f2440;
    --blue:#0077c8; --blue-bright:#0090e8; --blue-light:#7bb8e8;
    --blue-pale:#d6eaf8; --blue-bg:#f0f7fd;
    --ink:#1a2a3a; --gray:#4a5a6a; --gray-light:#8aa0b0; --line:#ccdde8;
    --white:#ffffff; --surface:#f5f9fc; --paper:#efe7db;
    --green:#2f8659; --green-soft:#dcf0e5;
    --red:#c85449; --red-soft:#fce9e6;
    --amber:#c9821f; --amber-soft:#fdf1da;
    --shadow-card:0 1px 3px rgba(60,50,40,.06), 0 6px 20px rgba(60,50,40,.08);
  }

  *{box-sizing:border-box}
  body{
    margin:0; background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink);
    font-family:'Open Sans',system-ui,sans-serif; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;
    -webkit-print-color-adjust:exact; print-color-adjust:exact;
  }
  .page{
    max-width:820px; margin:0 auto; background:var(--white);
    box-shadow:var(--shadow-card); border-radius:14px; overflow:hidden;
    margin-top:28px; margin-bottom:28px;
  }
  .pad{padding:34px 44px}

  h1,h2,h3,.eyebrow{font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif}
  h2.sec{font-weight:700; font-size:23px; color:var(--navy); margin:0 0 4px; letter-spacing:.2px}
  h3{font-weight:600; font-size:17px; color:var(--navy); margin:26px 0 6px}
  .eyebrow{font-size:12px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:1.5px; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--blue)}
  p{margin:0 0 14px}
  a{color:var(--blue)}
  strong{color:var(--ink)}

  /* ---- Cover ---- */
  .cover{
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--navy) 0%,var(--navy-deep) 100%);
    color:var(--white); text-align:center; padding:56px 44px 48px;
  }
  .cover .logo-plate{
    display:inline-block; background:var(--white); padding:16px 22px;
    border-radius:12px; margin-bottom:26px;
  }
  .cover .logo-plate img{display:block; height:52px}
  .cover h1{font-size:34px; font-weight:800; margin:0 0 6px; letter-spacing:.5px}
  .cover .kicker{font-size:14px; letter-spacing:3px; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--blue-light); font-weight:600}
  .cover .sub{font-family:'Open Sans',sans-serif; max-width:520px; margin:20px auto 0; color:#cfe0ef; font-size:15px}
  .cover .badge{
    display:inline-block; margin-top:26px; border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.3);
    border-radius:30px; padding:8px 20px; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:1px;
    text-transform:uppercase; font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:600; color:#e6f0f8;
  }

  /* ---- Shared pieces ---- */
  .lead{font-size:16px; color:var(--gray)}
  figure{margin:16px 0 4px}
  figure img{width:100%; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:10px; display:block; box-shadow:var(--shadow-card)}
  figcaption{font-size:12.5px; color:var(--gray-light); margin-top:7px; text-align:center; font-style:italic}

  .steps{counter-reset:step; margin:6px 0 4px; padding:0; list-style:none}
  .steps li{
    position:relative; padding:4px 0 12px 42px; margin:0;
  }
  .steps li::before{
    counter-increment:step; content:counter(step);
    position:absolute; left:0; top:2px; width:28px; height:28px;
    background:var(--blue); color:#fff; border-radius:50%;
    font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:700; font-size:14px;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;
  }

  .callout{
    border-left:4px solid var(--blue); background:var(--blue-bg);
    border-radius:8px; padding:14px 18px; margin:16px 0; font-size:14px;
  }
  .callout.tip{border-left-color:var(--green); background:var(--green-soft)}
  .callout.warn{border-left-color:var(--amber); background:var(--amber-soft)}
  .callout .lbl{font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:700; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.5px; text-transform:uppercase; display:block; margin-bottom:3px; color:var(--navy)}
  .fill{background:var(--amber-soft); border:1px dashed var(--amber); border-radius:5px; padding:1px 7px; font-size:13px; color:#8a5a10; font-weight:600; font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif}

  .divider{height:1px; background:var(--line); margin:34px 0}

  /* ---- Glossary ---- */
  .glossary{border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden}
  .glossary .row{display:flex; border-top:1px solid var(--line)}
  .glossary .row:first-child{border-top:none}
  .glossary .term{
    flex:0 0 190px; background:var(--surface); padding:11px 16px;
    font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:600; font-size:13.5px; color:var(--navy);
  }
  .glossary .def{padding:11px 16px; font-size:13.5px}

  /* ---- Status chips (mirror the app) ---- */
  .chip{display:inline-block; font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:700; font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.4px; padding:3px 11px; border-radius:20px; margin:2px 4px 2px 0}
  .chip.pre{background:var(--amber-soft); color:var(--amber)}
  .chip.active{background:var(--green-soft); color:var(--green)}
  .chip.overdue{background:var(--red-soft); color:var(--red)}
  .chip.declined{background:var(--surface); color:var(--gray)}

  /* ---- Supervisor add-on ---- */
  .addon{background:var(--navy); color:#e6f0f8; border-radius:14px; padding:30px 34px; margin:8px 0}
  .addon h2{color:#fff}
  .addon .eyebrow{color:var(--blue-light)}
  .addon strong{color:#fff}
  .addon figure img{border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.15)}
  .addon figcaption{color:#9fb6cc}
  .addon .callout{background:rgba(255,255,255,.06); border-left-color:var(--blue-light); color:#dce8f2}
  .addon .callout .lbl{color:#fff}

  .foot{text-align:center; color:var(--gray-light); font-size:12px; padding:8px 0 30px}

  /* ---- Print ---- */
  @media print{
    body{background:#fff}
    .page{box-shadow:none; margin:0; border-radius:0; max-width:none}
    .no-print{display:none}
    .section{page-break-inside:avoid}
    .cover{page-break-after:always}
    figure img{box-shadow:none}
  }
  .printbar{
    max-width:820px; margin:22px auto 0; text-align:right;
  }
  .printbtn{
    font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif; font-weight:600; font-size:13px;
    background:var(--navy); color:#fff; border:none; padding:10px 20px;
    border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; letter-spacing:.3px;
  }
  .printbtn:hover{background:var(--navy-deep)}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="printbar no-print">
  <button class="printbtn" onclick="window.print()">🖨️ Print / Save as PDF</button>
</div>

<!-- ============ COVER ============ -->
<div class="page">
  <div class="cover">
    <span class="logo-plate"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Endeavors"></span>
    <div class="kicker">DCM Case Tracker</div>
    <h1>Welcome to the Team</h1>
    <div class="sub">Your friendly get-started guide to the Endeavors Disaster Case Management tracker — the tool you'll use every day to walk survivors through their recovery.</div>
    <div class="badge">DR-4879 · Texas Hill Country Flood Response</div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ 1. WELCOME / WHAT THIS IS ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Start Here</div>
    <h2 class="sec">What is this app?</h2>
    <p class="lead">The DCM Case Tracker is where every survivor's story lives while we help them recover.</p>
    <p>DCM stands for <strong>Disaster Case Management</strong> — the work of guiding a family, step by step, from the day of the disaster to the day their recovery plan is complete. This app keeps all of that in one place so nothing falls through the cracks: who you've talked to, what step each case is on, what's due, and what still needs doing.</p>
    <p>Instead of juggling spreadsheets and sticky notes, you'll have a single home base that quietly keeps score for you. It even builds your weekly report as you work, so reporting stops being a Friday scramble.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="lbl">The big idea</span>
      Every survivor gets a <strong>case</strong>. Each case moves through a set of <strong>steps</strong>. Your job is to keep cases moving and to <strong>log every contact</strong> along the way. Everything else in this guide is just the how-to for that.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="divider" style="margin-left:44px;margin-right:44px"></div>

  <!-- ============ 2. FIRST 10 MINUTES ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Day One</div>
    <h2 class="sec">Your first ten minutes</h2>
    <p>Your supervisor or the admin will create your account before you start. When it's ready, you'll get an email invite. Here's the whole warm-up:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open the app at <a href="https://masoriano2024-jpg.github.io/EndCaseLog/"><strong>masoriano2024-jpg.github.io/EndCaseLog</strong></a> in your browser (Chrome or Edge work great).</li>
      <li>Sign in with your <strong>work email and password</strong>. First time in? Use the temporary password from your invite, then set your own.</li>
      <li>You'll land on the <strong>Dashboard</strong> — your home base. Up top on the right, check that your <strong>name, role, and office</strong> look right (for example, "Case Manager · San Angelo").</li>
      <li>If anything there is wrong, don't fix it yourself — tell the <strong>Admin</strong> so they can set your role and office correctly.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Dashboard with the Daily Briefing panel">
      <figcaption>The Dashboard. The blue <em>Daily Briefing</em> panel is your at-a-glance count of everything on your plate today.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="callout tip">
      <span class="lbl">Reassurance</span>
      You can't break anything by clicking around. Take a few minutes to explore the five tabs across the top — <strong>Dashboard, Roster, Weekly Report, Self-Audit, Notes</strong>. We'll cover each one below.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="divider" style="margin-left:44px;margin-right:44px"></div>

  <!-- ============ 3. DAILY WORK ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="eyebrow">The Everyday</div>
    <h2 class="sec">Your daily work</h2>
    <p>Ninety percent of your day is four simple actions. Here's each one.</p>

    <h3>① Start a new case</h3>
    <p>When a new survivor comes to you, give them a case. On the Dashboard, click the blue <strong>+ Start New Case</strong> button.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Enter the <strong>Survivor ID</strong> — this is the <strong>number that TCR generates</strong> for the survivor, not their name.</li>
      <li>Optionally add initials<strong>name or note</strong> to help you remember who's who. This stays private to you.</li>
      <li>Confirm the <strong>date assigned</strong>, then click <strong>Begin Timeline</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Start New Case window">
      <figcaption>Starting a case takes about ten seconds. The name field is optional and stays private to you.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="lbl">Keep PII off the site</span>
      Always identify survivors by their <strong>TCR-generated ID number</strong>. We want to keep <strong>PII</strong> (personally identifiable information — names, addresses, social security numbers, and the like) off the site. The optional name field is the one exception, and it stays private to you.
    </div>

    <h3>② Log a contact</h3>
    <p>This is the single most important habit. <strong>Every time</strong> you reach out to a survivor — call, text, email, or in person — log it. Click the blue <strong>Log a Contact</strong> button (bottom-right corner of any screen).</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Enter the <strong>Survivor ID</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick the <strong>method</strong>: Call, Text, Email, or In Person.</li>
      <li>The date and time fill in automatically — adjust if you're logging it later.</li>
      <li>Choose a <strong>result</strong> and jot a quick <strong>note</strong>. If you <strong>left a message</strong>, it's important to pick that result — a voicemail is a real outreach attempt, and choosing "left a message" keeps your records (and your weekly numbers) accurate.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Log It</strong>. Done.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Log Contact Attempt window">
      <figcaption>Logging a contact. Even a "no answer" counts — log it, because attempts matter too.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="lbl">Why it matters</span>
      Your <strong>Weekly Report builds itself</strong> from these logs. If you don't log a contact, it didn't happen as far as reporting is concerned. When in doubt, log it.
    </div>

    <h3>③ Move a case through its steps</h3>
    <p>Each case card on the Dashboard shows where that survivor is — their <strong>status</strong>, their <strong>current step</strong>, and whether anything is <strong>overdue</strong>. As you complete work, walk the case forward.</p>
    <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;margin:6px 0 10px">
      <li style="margin-bottom:6px"><strong>Next Step →</strong> moves the case to the next step in its timeline.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:6px"><strong>← Previous</strong> steps back if you moved too far.</li>
      <li><strong>Close</strong> finishes a case when the survivor's plan is done (or if they decline).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You'll see little colored tags that tell you a case's status at a glance:</p>
    <p>
      <span class="chip pre">Pre-Opened</span>
      <span class="chip active">Active</span>
      <span class="chip overdue">Overdue 5d</span>
      <span class="chip declined">Declined</span>
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Case cards on the Dashboard showing steps and status">
      <figcaption>Case cards. The colored stripe on the left and the tags tell you a case's health at a glance — red means it needs attention.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="lbl">"Made Contact" is a turning point</span>
      Clicking <strong>Made Contact</strong> at any time <strong>interrupts Path A (Outreach)</strong>. At that moment you have two choices:
      <ul style="margin:8px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li style="margin-bottom:5px">The survivor <strong>declines service</strong> → <strong>close the case</strong>.</li>
        <li>The survivor <strong>accepts service</strong> → <strong>continue the process</strong>, which pushes them onto <strong>Path B (Intake)</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>④ Keep your notes &amp; to-dos</h3>
    <p>The <strong>Notes</strong> tab is your personal command center. Three things live here:</p>
    <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;margin:6px 0 10px">
      <li style="margin-bottom:6px"><strong>My To-Do List</strong> — your own reminders, just for you.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:6px"><strong>Case Notes</strong> — the running story of a case. Enter a Survivor ID to read or add notes. <em>(These are internal notes — different from a contact log.)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Client To-Do List</strong> — tasks tied to a specific survivor, with a due date (e.g. "Submit contractor estimate").</li>
    </ul>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Notes tab with to-do lists and case notes">
      <figcaption>The Notes tab keeps your personal reminders, a survivor's case notes, and their to-do tasks all in one place.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Where everything lives: the Roster</h3>
    <p>The <strong>Roster</strong> tab is the full list of your survivors by ID. Click any name to open their complete profile — status, full contact history, audit records, case notes, and open tasks — all on one screen. When you need the whole picture of one survivor, this is where you go.</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Roster tab showing a survivor's full profile">
      <figcaption>A survivor's profile in the Roster. Everything you've logged about them, gathered in one view.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="divider" style="margin-left:44px;margin-right:44px"></div>

  <!-- ============ 4. WORDS YOU'LL HEAR ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Speak the Language</div>
    <h2 class="sec">Words you'll hear</h2>
    <p>A quick plain-English glossary so nothing sounds like alphabet soup.</p>
    <div class="glossary">
      <div class="row"><div class="term">DCM</div><div class="def">Disaster Case Management — helping a survivor through recovery, step by step.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Survivor / Client</div><div class="def">The person we're helping. Tracked by an <strong>ID number</strong>, not a name, to protect their privacy.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Case</div><div class="def">One survivor's whole recovery journey inside the app.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Step</div><div class="def">A single stage in that journey (e.g. "Phone call within 24 hours"). Cases move step by step.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Path A / Path B</div><div class="def">Two kinds of timelines. <strong>Path A</strong> is Outreach; <strong>Path B</strong> is Intake. Which one a case follows depends on where the survivor is in the process.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Contact Log</div><div class="def">A record of one attempt to reach a survivor. The heartbeat of the app.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Case Note</div><div class="def">An internal note about a case — your written memory. Not the same as a contact log.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Pre-Opened</div><div class="def">A case that's started but not yet fully active — early outreach stage.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">Self-Audit</div><div class="def">A checklist to make sure a case has all its required paperwork and info — see below.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="term">TCR</div><div class="def">Texas Community Resource — the wider system our recovery plans connect to.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Self-Audit checklist</h3>
    <p>The <strong>Self-Audit</strong> tab lets you check a case for completeness — is the paperwork in order, is the intake info filled out, are the release forms current? Load a survivor's ID and work down the checklist. The percentage at the top climbs as you check things off, so you can spot gaps before they become problems.</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Self-Audit checklist for a client">
      <figcaption>The Self-Audit checklist. A quick way to make sure a case is fully documented and audit-ready.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="divider" style="margin-left:44px;margin-right:44px"></div>

  <!-- ============ 5. STUCK? HELP ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="eyebrow">You're Not Alone</div>
    <h2 class="sec">Stuck? Here's help</h2>
    <p>Everybody has questions in week one. Here's where to turn, in order:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Your supervisor is the number one place to get answers.</strong> Most "how do I…" questions get sorted out in thirty seconds — start there.</li>
      <li><strong>Found a bug or something confusing?</strong> Click the <strong>🐞 Report an Issue</strong> button in the bottom-left corner of the app. It sends a support ticket straight to the admin — describe what happened and, if it's about a case, include the Survivor ID.</li>
      <li><strong>Account or access problems</strong> (can't log in, wrong role or office): contact the Admin, <strong>Miguel Soriano</strong> — <a href="mailto:miguel.soriano@endeavors.org">miguel.soriano@endeavors.org</a>.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout tip">
      <span class="lbl">Golden rule</span>
      When in doubt, <strong>log the contact and write the note</strong>. It's much easier to clean up a little extra detail later than to reconstruct something that was never written down.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ 6. SUPERVISOR ADD-ON ============ -->
  <div class="pad section">
    <div class="addon">
      <div class="eyebrow">For Supervisors Only</div>
      <h2 class="sec">Supervisor add-on</h2>
      <p style="color:#dce8f2">If you're a case manager, you can stop here — the rest of this page is for supervisors. As a supervisor you see everything a case manager sees, <strong>plus</strong> the wider view of your office.</p>
      <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;margin:10px 0 4px">
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px">📊 <strong>Office-wide view</strong> — you see the cases for your whole office, not just your own, so you can spot where a survivor might be slipping.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px">📈 <strong>Weekly Report for the team</strong> — the same live report a case manager sees, but across your office. Export it to Excel anytime for reporting up the chain.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px">👥 <strong>Your team</strong> — case managers are assigned to you, so their work rolls up into your view.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Weekly Report with summary cards and contact log">
        <figcaption>The Weekly Report. Summary cards up top, the full contact log below, and a one-click export to Excel.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="lbl">Before go-live</span>
        A few permission settings still need to be tested from a real case-manager and supervisor login (not just the admin account). If something looks visible that shouldn't be, flag it to the Admin, <strong>Miguel Soriano</strong> (<a href="mailto:miguel.soriano@endeavors.org" style="color:var(--blue-light)">miguel.soriano@endeavors.org</a>).
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="foot">
    Endeavors · DCM Case Tracker · Welcome Packet &nbsp;·&nbsp; Draft v1 · July 2026
  </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>
